Human sleep is made up of several stages. Scientists can tell which stage we are in by looking at the electrical signals on the scalp, a method called electroencephalography (EEG). We can dream in all stages, but the dreams that feel the most vivid happen during REM sleep, which stands for Rapid Eye Movement. In REM sleep, the brain shuts down most of the body’s muscles (except the eye muscles) so we don’t act out our dreams. Activities like sleepwalking usually happen during non‑REM stages, when one part of the brain is awake while the rest is still asleep. Talking in your sleep also occurs in non‑REM stages because it requires the brain to control quick muscle movements that are not stopped.

