The measurement of surface slope, based on almost six hundred trials, proved to be an exceedingly delicate procedure, raising doubts about the usefulness of the results. This uncertainty would impact every formula that incorporated the slope. The water surface, when averaged over its oscillations, was found to be essentially level across, contrary to some writers who claimed it to be convex. Five hundred sixty‑five sets of vertical velocity data were combined into forty‑six series. All forty‑six averaged curves were relatively flat and convex downstream, except near an irregular bank, and they resembled parabolas with horizontal axes. The data only allowed rough determination of the parameters. The line of maximum velocity usually lay below the service level and dipped in a rectangular channel from the center outward to about mid‑depth near the banks. This depression seemed unrelated to depth, slope, velocity, or wind; it was likely caused by the continuous surface retardation from the air itself, while wind had little effect due to its short duration. On any vertical section, the velocity at mid‑depth exceeded the mean, whereas the bed velocity was the lowest.

