Mother had dark, shining skin and black hair with a small wave. She looked kind and patient, but she also had a quiet determination. Our family were small farmers on Egremont Plain, between Great Barrington and Sheffield, Massachusetts. The land was too small to support a big family, so we were always poor. I never remember being cold or hungry, but I remember my mother worrying about shoes, coal, and sometimes flour during winter. A new suit was a big event! Around the time I was born, the family was moving from farming to working as hired help. Some people left for the West, others went to the city to work as cooks or barbers. Mother worked in a house in Great Barrington for a few years. After a disappointed love affair with a cousin who went to California, she married Alfred Du Bois and moved to a town by the golden river where I was born.

