 Because the Earth is getting warmer, there is less snow and ice. The warm water around the North and South Poles melts more, so the ice at the sea’s surface melts more in winter and glaciers shrink. This leaves more open water that isn’t frozen. The extra open water lets tiny sea plants called microalgae grow for a longer time. When they grow, they use more carbon dioxide from the air, which helps clean the atmosphere. The microalgae also give food to fish and other sea animals. When these animals grow, they store the carbon in their bodies. This stored carbon in the ocean is called blue carbon. When animals die, some of the blue carbon sinks to the bottom of the sea and stays there, keeping it out of the air. This process is called sequestration. Warmer climates make more blue carbon in the polar regions, which is a good thing because it helps cool the Earth. This is an example of negative feedback that helps fight global warming. This article tells us what we have learned from studying blue carbon.

