During the earliest stages of human development, the auditory system of most newborns remains inactive, a phenomenon that persists for several days following birth. It was not until the fourth day that Professor Preyer was able to observe any measurable evidence of hearing in his own child. By the eleventh week of life the infant had begun to orient his head toward external sounds; by the sixteenth week this orientation had evolved into a response that was as swift and consistent as a reflexive movement. At eight months—well before the onset of articulate speech—the child was capable of distinguishing between a musical tone and an arbitrary noise, a perceptual ability he demonstrated by smiling upon hearing the timbre of a piano. After the first year of life he derived satisfaction from striking the piano himself. By the twenty‑first month he began to dance in time with music, and by the twenty‑fourth month he had progressed to the imitation of entire songs. Independent observers have reported that certain infants are able to sing with accurate pitch, and even reproduce a melody, as early as nine months of age. One such infant was documented singing while asleep at that age, and by nineteen months he was able to maintain tempo with his hands while singing a simple air.

