 Of all orchid genera, none is as distinct or as diverse in size, shape, structure, and color as Masdevallia. The first species, M. uniflora, was collected near a century ago by Ruiz and Pavon in Mexico; it remains largely unknown to most modern growers. When John Lindley published his 1836 book *Genera and Species*, only three Masdevallia species were documented. Twenty‑five years later, his *Folio Orchidaceæ* listed almost forty species, and today our gardens host perhaps a hundred different kinds. Explorers still report the beautiful varieties that flourish high on the cloud‑covered Andes and Cordilleras of the New World. Masdevallia is found only in the Western hemisphere. Like birds and mammals, orchid distributions often mirror the pattern of one hemisphere, so the search for a comparable genus elsewhere usually yields a familiar counterpart.

