 During induction, magnets only affect the thin cloud of air around the path where static electricity travels. This cloud, which is like a sheath of heated air filled with tiny metal particles that come from the flowing electric charge, is exactly the same as the glowing spark we see. Even though the currents that build up inside the coil change direction, the instrument shows that the currents that go through the break all move in the same direction. Those reverse currents are stopped as they travel. To collect them, you must lower the gas pressure of the material that lets the current pass, increase its ability to conduct electricity, or add a very strong metal path that resists the flow. Using this last method, I managed to separate, into two separate circuits, the direct induced currents and the reverse induced ones.

