 There had been very strange weather in Colchester during October 1705. On the 13th, an unusually early cold spell came and lasted three days. Then the weather warmed for two days. The river froze during the cold spell, but the ice melted during the warm days, until the 19th when it was breaking up and heading out to sea. That night, a very cold wind blew, colder than before. It stopped the broken ice, piled it up in odd shapes, and made it look like a rough glacier. After the cold wind, a heavy snowstorm followed. Colchester was covered with three feet of snow. The village roads were clear, but farther out they were not. It was strange to have the river closed so early, and the winter supplies from New London and Norwich had not yet arrived. Even Mr. Chapin, the storekeeper, had only a few staples he usually kept in plenty.

