Many people know Nemo, the bright orange and white clownfish from the movie “Finding Nemo,” and his friend Dory, a blue and yellow surgeonfish. Both of these fish live in coral reefs and are loved for their pretty colors. Scientists are curious about how the fish people think are the most beautiful help the reef. They want to know if the pretty fish are as helpful to the reef as the fish that look less pretty. To find out which fish people liked best, the scientists ran an online survey. The survey showed two pictures of tropical fish at a time, and the viewer had to choose the fish they thought looked best. About eight thousand people took the survey, and each person compared twenty pairs of fish. The survey gave a list of 116 different fish species, ranked from the ones people thought were the ugliest to the ones they thought were the most beautiful, based on the number of votes.

