The Hui Aloha Aina for Women and the Hui Aloha Aina for Men started a big petition campaign. They wanted to show the U.S. government that many native Hawaiians did not want Hawaii to be part of the U.S. Between September 11 and October 2, 1897, they asked people to sign petitions at meetings on all five main Hawaiian islands. The petition, titled "Petition Against Annexation" and written in Hawaiian and English, was signed by 21,269 native Hawaiians. That was more than half of the 39,000 native Hawaiians and mixed-blood people counted in the Hawaiian census that year.

