 Living things are different from things that are not alive. It is usually easy to see what is alive and what is not, except for very small living things that you cannot see with your eyes and for pale, dull-looking plants that look dead. Here are some traits of living things. Some non-living things can have a few of these traits. Living things can change and grow. Volcanoes also change and grow. Living things can move. The wind moves air, and water always moves downhill. You might want to know how plants move. They grow, and sometimes move faster than that, when they respond to things like the sun or water. For example, sunflowers turn around during the day so they always face the sun. Another example is that if a plant is tipped over, it will try to point upward. Living things can make more of themselves. That is, they can produce copies of themselves again and again. This is the most important difference between living and non-living things. To make more of themselves, living things need food, which are chemicals and energy sources that help them build the parts they need to make new copies. In this process, living things must get rid of waste.

