When you dig a tree out of the ground, you cut off all the roots that lie outside a circle a few feet in diameter. The tree is then reset with its full crown of branches. If you have ever seen a tree knocked over by a tornado, you know how the roots can run close to the surface and stretch far away. When a tree’s roots are cut off only two or three feet from the trunk, most of the tiny feeding roots are gone. If you leave the top of the tree in place, the buds may try to grow in spring, but without enough root support they won’t get the sap they need, and the tree will die. However, if you remove the entire top and leave only a bare trunk, the tree can often be transplanted successfully. In that case, the tree acts like a large cutting. The remaining roots are enough to feed the few shoots that start from the top, and the tree’s growth above and below ground stays in balance.

